Q 1: Explain JVM architecture and justify why Java is platform-independent.
Answer: JVM consists of Class Loader, Bytecode Verifier, Memory Areas (Method Area, Heap, Java Stacks, PC Registers, Native
Method Stacks), and Execution Engine (Interpreter + JIT Compiler). Java source code compiles into platform-independent Bytecode.
Because different OS platforms run platform-specific JVM implementations that accept the identical bytecode, Java is
platform-independent.
Q 2: Differentiate between JDK, JRE, and JVM.
Answer: JVM is the runtime engine executing bytecode. JRE contains JVM plus standard class libraries. JDK is the software
development kit containing JRE plus development tools (javac, javadoc, jar).

